<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/maven-v4_0_0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
    <parent>
        <groupId>de.cuioss.sheriff.gateway</groupId>
        <artifactId>api-sheriff-parent</artifactId>
        <version>0.2.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>api-sheriff-build-parent</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>
    <name>API Sheriff Build Parent</name>
    <description>Published build parent for downstream deployers. A third party inherits from this POM, sets one
        property, and their build produces an API Sheriff image carrying a custom context path. Adopting a new API
        Sheriff release is a &lt;parent&gt;&lt;version&gt; bump and nothing else.
    </description>

    <!-- WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS.

         The three context-path keys (quarkus.http.root-path, quarkus.http.non-application-root-path,
         quarkus.management.root-path) are Quarkus BUILD-TIME keys, fixed at augmentation. The matching
         environment variables are inert against an already-built image, so a custom context path ALWAYS
         costs a rebuild — see api-sheriff/src/main/resources/application.properties and
         doc/user/context-path.adoc. This module does not pretend otherwise; it makes the mandatory
         rebuild a ONE-PROPERTY operation for someone who never clones this repository.

         This module carries NO Java and builds NO artifact. It exists to be PUBLISHED rather than built:
         the reactor walks through it as a near no-op (quarkus-maven-plugin's build and generate-code
         goals both short-circuit on pom packaging with "Type of the artifact is POM, skipping build
         goal"), and the value is delivered entirely by inheritance on a consumer's side.

         It declares exactly four things a consumer needs, and nothing else:
           (1) the consumer-facing context-path knob and its mapping onto the stock Quarkus keys,
           (2) the api-sheriff dependency plus the Quarkus platform BOM,
           (3) the quarkus-maven-plugin binding, with the main class pinned,
           (4) the native profile.
         Plus one defensive concern: neutralising this project's own hygiene toolchain, which a consumer
         would otherwise inherit transitively through cui-java-parent. -->

    <properties>
        <!-- ==== (1) The consumer-facing knob ============================================== -->
        <!-- A consumer overrides sheriff.context-path (and optionally sheriff.management-context-path)
             in its OWN <properties>. Maven merges child properties over parent ones and interpolates
             the effective model, so the child's value reaches augmentation through the mapping below.
             Defaults are the same '/' and '/q' that api-sheriff's application.properties declares, so
             an inheriting build that overrides nothing produces the stock image.

             THIS MAPPING IS UNCONDITIONAL, DELIBERATELY, and it is NOT an inconsistency with the
             -D-activated `context-path` / `management-context-path` profiles in api-sheriff/pom.xml.
             MAVEN ACTIVATES A PROFILE BY PROPERTY FROM USER PROPERTIES ONLY — `-D` on the command line
             or a settings.xml entry. It NEVER activates one from a POM <properties> entry, because POM
             properties are resolved only after profile activation has already been decided. A consumer
             sets a POM property, so a profile here would silently never fire and the override would be
             lost with no error. Do NOT "simplify" this into an <activation><property> profile: that is
             the single most likely edit a later reader will attempt, and it breaks the whole module.
             It does not, however, leave the build green: BuildParentContractTest reads the top-level
             <properties> of this POM only, so moving the mapping into a profile makes the carrier
             lookup null and its assertion fires.

             quarkus-maven-plugin honours a plain Maven property as build-time Quarkus configuration.
             That is not speculative: api-sheriff/pom.xml's `native` profile already binds
             quarkus.native.enabled and quarkus.ssl.native exactly this way.

             THE TARGET OF THE MAPPING IS A CARRIER KEY, NOT quarkus.http.root-path ITSELF, and that
             is measured rather than stylistic. Two facts compose:

               (i)  quarkus-maven-plugin forwards properties through
                    getBuildSystemProperties(quarkusOnly), which keeps ONLY `quarkus.`-prefixed
                    names. `sheriff.context-path` is filtered out and never reaches augmentation —
                    which is why this mapping exists at all rather than the consumer's property
                    being read directly.
               (ii) The survivors land at ordinal 100, while api-sheriff's own packaged
                    application.properties is ordinal 250 and wins for every key it declares. A
                    mapping onto quarkus.http.root-path would therefore be overridden by the very
                    declaration it means to change: with that shape, a build carrying
                    sheriff.context-path=/gw produced ZERO occurrences of `/gw` in
                    generated-bytecode.jar.

             api-sheriff's application.properties instead declares
             `quarkus.http.root-path=${quarkus.sheriff-context-path:/}` — an expression over the
             carrier. The 250-ordinal declaration still wins, but resolves the carrier from whatever
             source supplies it: this mapping at ordinal 100, or the inline default when a consumer
             overrides nothing. So do NOT "correct" the two target names below to the stock Quarkus
             keys; that reads more obvious and silently stops the parent working. It does not keep
             the build green either: BuildParentContractTest asserts both directions of exactly that
             edit: the carrier names must be present and the two stock Quarkus keys must be absent
             from this POM, so the substitution turns it red. Quarkus owns no such keys, so the
             accepted cost of this shape was that it
             would log them as unrecognized configuration at boot; that warning was not in fact
             observed at JVM or native augmentation or at container boot — measured, not
             guaranteed. -->
        <sheriff.context-path>/</sheriff.context-path>
        <sheriff.management-context-path>/q</sheriff.management-context-path>
        <quarkus.sheriff-context-path>${sheriff.context-path}</quarkus.sheriff-context-path>
        <quarkus.sheriff-mgmt-path>${sheriff.management-context-path}</quarkus.sheriff-mgmt-path>

        <!-- ==== (3a) The pinned application entry point ==================================== -->
        <!-- Pinned rather than left to @QuarkusMain discovery, so a consumer's augmentation does not
             depend on the annotation being found through a dependency's Jandex index.

             The key spelling was VERIFIED against the pinned Quarkus 3.39.1, not assumed:
             io.quarkus.deployment.pkg.PackageConfig is annotated @ConfigMapping(prefix = "quarkus.package")
             and declares `Optional<String> mainClass()` at the root of that mapping — so the key is
             quarkus.package.main-class. It is NOT quarkus.package.jar.main-class; the 3.x restructuring
             moved `type`, `compress` and friends under `jar`, but left `main-class` where it was.
             Re-verify against PackageConfig on any Quarkus platform bump. -->
        <quarkus.package.main-class>de.cuioss.sheriff.gateway.ApiSheriffApplication</quarkus.package.main-class>

        <!-- ==== (2a) The Quarkus platform version ========================================== -->
        <!-- Declared as a real property, on purpose, even though this POM also inherits it from
             api-sheriff-parent. Maven does not propagate properties out of an IMPORTED BOM, and
             quarkus-maven-plugin needs the value as a build extension — which is why every Quarkus
             consumer normally has to declare it locally. Solving that once, here, on the consumer's
             behalf is part of what this parent is for.

             The duplication is guarded rather than merely tolerated:
             .claude/skills/release/check-quarkus-alignment.py rglobs every pom.xml under the
             repository (not just the reactor's, so this non-reactor example is covered too) and reports
             a property declared at two different values as "declared inconsistently", exiting 2 — which
             the release gate treats as blocking. So this value drifting from the reactor root's cannot
             reach a release, and no new drift mechanism was added for it. Keep it equal to the
             <version.quarkus> in ../pom.xml. -->
        <version.quarkus>3.39.1</version.quarkus>

        <!-- ==== Consumer hygiene is the consumer's business ================================ -->
        <!-- A consumer inheriting this parent transitively inherits api-sheriff-parent and thereby
             de.cuioss:cui-java-parent, which binds license-header stamping, OpenRewrite, checkstyle,
             spotbugs, pmd/cpd, enforcer and jacoco. Those are THIS project's internal
             hygiene, not a third party's: a deployer who inherits this POM to rebuild a gateway image
             has not opted into CUI's code-quality toolchain and would be handed failing builds over
             conventions they never adopted. Every entry below is therefore switched off as an INHERITED
             DEFAULT — a consumer that does want one re-enables it in its own <properties>.

             The enumeration is not invented here. It is the list deployment/pom.xml:32-45 already
             established for a packaging:pom module that should build nothing; the property NAMES are
             taken from there rather than re-derived.

             Four of that list are deliberately LEFT ALONE, so the consumer's own build still works and
             this module still ships:
               - maven.compiler.skip — the consumer compiles its own sources.
               - skipTests           — the consumer runs its own tests.
               - maven.deploy.skip   — this module must reach Maven Central. Note that the EFFECTIVE
                                       publish switch under cui-java-parent is skipPublishing, not this
                                       one (deployment/pom.xml:47-52 records why).
               - skipPublishing      — likewise: unset, so this module publishes.
             A fifth, maven.install.skip, is also left alone, and that is a considered departure from
             deployment/pom.xml rather than an omission. deployment sets it because that module must
             never be consumable at all; this one is the opposite — a PUBLISHED parent whose whole
             purpose is to be resolved by someone else. Setting it would stop `mvn install` putting this
             POM in the local repository, and would silently stop every inheriting consumer installing
             their own artifact. It is a lifecycle switch, not part of the hygiene toolchain named
             above. -->
        <maven.javadoc.skip>true</maven.javadoc.skip>
        <checkstyle.skip>true</checkstyle.skip>
        <spotbugs.skip>true</spotbugs.skip>
        <pmd.skip>true</pmd.skip>
        <cpd.skip>true</cpd.skip>
        <jacoco.skip>true</jacoco.skip>
        <license.skip>true</license.skip>
        <skip.openrewrite>true</skip.openrewrite>

        <!-- ==== TWO OF THAT LIST ARE SECURITY CONTROLS, NOT STYLE CHECKS ==================== -->
        <!-- Separated from the block above deliberately. The enumeration was taken from
             deployment/pom.xml, and it is correct THERE because that module compiles nothing and has
             no dependency graph to scan. This module is the opposite case by explicit design:
             maven.compiler.skip and skipTests are LEFT ALONE precisely because the consumer compiles
             its own sources and runs its own tests — so an inheriting build carries its own
             dependencies and its own Quarkus extensions, a graph API Sheriff's upstream scan never
             saw. Switching these two off as inherited defaults therefore removes a control from the
             consumer's build rather than from a module that had nothing to control.

               - dependency-check.skip — the property name OWASP Dependency-Check reads, the
                 known-vulnerable-dependency (CVE) scan. OWASP A06 Vulnerable and Outdated Components
                 / A03-2025 Software Supply Chain Failures.
               - enforcer.skip — maven-enforcer, which under cui-java-parent carries the
                 dependency-convergence and banned-dependency guards; without it a transitively
                 downgraded library can silently win the consumer's resolution.

             THE TWO ARE NOT SYMMETRIC, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
             maven-enforcer-plugin IS bound by the inherited chain — cui-parent-pom declares it in
             <build><plugins> (managed above it in <pluginManagement>) — so enforcer.skip genuinely
             suppresses a plugin that would otherwise run, and setting it `false` genuinely restores
             it. org.owasp:dependency-check-maven is bound NOWHERE: neither "dependency-check" nor
             "owasp" occurs anywhere in cui-parent-pom, cui-java-bom or cui-java-parent (checked
             against the resolved POMs at both 1.5.10 and 1.5.11), and no POM in this repository binds
             it either. dependency-check.skip is therefore INHERITED-BUT-UNBOUND: it is this
             project's own convention, carried for consistency with deployment/pom.xml and pinned by
             BuildParentContractTest, and NOTHING READS IT. Setting it `false` enables no scan.

             Both stay DEFAULTED OFF, but for DIFFERENT reasons, and those reasons are stated
             separately on purpose: a single shared sentence here previously justified both with
             dependency-check's rationale alone, and neither half of it is true of enforcer, which
             needs no key and costs seconds. A control riding a justification that belongs to its
             sibling is indistinguishable from one nobody re-examined.

               - dependency-check.skip stays off because the scan needs an NVD API key and adds
                 minutes to every consumer build. Were the plugin ever bound here, defaulting it ON
                 would fail a consumer on a missing credential rather than on a finding — which is
                 also why binding it is not the answer to the paragraph above.
               - enforcer.skip stays off because dependencyConvergence is an inherited CUI opinion
                 about a dependency graph this parent never sees, and a deployer who never adopted
                 CUI's toolchain should not meet it as a first-build failure. That is the same
                 argument, and the same class of inherited-CUI-default breakage, as the
                 Automatic-Module-Name block twenty lines below: an upstream default that fails a
                 non-CUI consumer's very first `mvn package` for a reason that has nothing to do
                 with API Sheriff.

             What is NOT acceptable is leaving that consequence invisible — a security control's
             removal must be explicit and named (secure-by-default / complete mediation), which is
             exactly what folding it into a ten-entry "hygiene" list defeats. Nor is offering a
             re-enablement route that does nothing: a consumer who sets dependency-check.skip to
             `false` and believes they have turned CVE scanning on is worse off than one who knows
             there is none.

             So the two routes differ. enforcer.skip is re-enabled by setting the same property to
             `false` in the consumer's own <properties>, and that works. For vulnerability scanning
             there is no property to flip: the consumer scans the image they produce (Trivy/Grype or
             equivalent) in their own pipeline, or binds dependency-check-maven themselves. Either
             way, whoever inherits this parent OWNS the vulnerability scanning of the image they
             build — API Sheriff's own upstream scan never saw their graph.
             doc/user/downstream-parent.adoc states the same thing on the consumer's side. Do NOT
             quietly move these two back into the block above. -->
        <dependency-check.skip>true</dependency-check.skip>
        <enforcer.skip>true</enforcer.skip>

        <!-- ==== A valid automatic module name for the consumer's jar ======================= -->
        <!-- Not a stylistic default: without it the parent's one-property promise is false for most
             real consumers, and this was found by BUILDING build-parent/example rather than by
             reading POMs. cui-java-parent writes an <Automatic-Module-Name> manifest entry from
             ${project.groupId}.${project.artifactId}, and a Java module name may not contain a
             hyphen — so `com.example:my-gateway` yields `com.example.my-gateway` and
             maven-jar-plugin aborts the build with

                 Error assembling JAR: Invalid automatic module name: 'com.example.my-gateway'

             Since most artifactIds are hyphenated, the inherited default fails a consumer on their
             very first `mvn package` for a reason that has nothing to do with API Sheriff. A neutral,
             valid literal is supplied instead. It is a manifest attribute on the consumer's own
             application jar, which Quarkus repackages into quarkus-app/app/ and never loads as a
             JPMS module, so the value is inert; a consumer who does care overrides this property
             exactly as api-sheriff/pom.xml already does for itself. -->
        <maven.jar.plugin.automatic.module.name>de.cuioss.sheriff.gateway.consumer</maven.jar.plugin.automatic.module.name>
    </properties>

    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <!-- ==== (2b) The Quarkus platform ============================================== -->
            <!-- Imported here as well as in api-sheriff-parent so this POM states the platform it
                 binds a consumer to, rather than leaving it to be discovered up the parent chain. -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
                <artifactId>quarkus-bom</artifactId>
                <version>${version.quarkus}</version>
                <type>pom</type>
                <scope>import</scope>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>

    <dependencies>
        <!-- ==== (2c) The application ==================================================== -->
        <!-- Declared as a real <dependency> rather than only managed, so a consumer declares NO
             dependency at all: inheriting this parent is what puts the gateway on the classpath.

             THE VERSION IS ${project.parent.version}, NOT ${project.version}, AND THAT IS MEASURED
             RATHER THAN STYLISTIC. An inherited <dependencies> entry is interpolated against the
             CHILD's effective model, not against the model of the POM that authored it. With
             ${project.version} the example resolved
             `de.cuioss.sheriff.gateway:api-sheriff:jar:1.0.0-SNAPSHOT` — the CONSUMER's own version —
             and the build failed with "Could not find artifact". ${project.parent.version} resolves,
             in the consumer's model, to the version of the parent it inherits: this POM's. That is
             precisely the promise being made — bump <parent><version> and the application version
             moves with it — expressed in the one form that actually holds under inheritance.

             Known boundary: a consumer that interposes its OWN aggregator parent between its module
             and this POM shifts what `project.parent` refers to. Such a consumer sets the version
             explicitly, exactly as it would for any other managed dependency. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.cuioss.sheriff.gateway</groupId>
            <artifactId>api-sheriff</artifactId>
            <version>${project.parent.version}</version>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!-- ==== (3b) The Quarkus build binding ========================================= -->
            <!-- BOUND, not merely managed: a consumer's `mvn package` must augment without the
                 consumer declaring a plugin. The version and the three goals are stated explicitly
                 rather than left to the inherited pluginManagement entry, so this binding is legible
                 in the one file a consumer reads.

                 Running on this module itself is harmless: both mojos short-circuit on pom packaging
                 ("Type of the artifact is POM, skipping build goal"), so the reactor leg costs nothing. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>io.quarkus</groupId>
                <artifactId>quarkus-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${version.quarkus}</version>
                <extensions>true</extensions>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>build</goal>
                            <goal>generate-code</goal>
                            <goal>generate-code-tests</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

    <profiles>
        <!-- ==== (4) The native profile ==================================================== -->
        <!-- Mirrors api-sheriff/pom.xml's `native` profile property-for-property, so `-Pnative` works
             for a consumer BY INHERITANCE (profiles are inherited by child POMs) rather than by
             copy-paste into every downstream POM.

             The two sets must not drift into producing differently-configured native images:
             BuildParentContractTest asserts parity between this profile and api-sheriff/pom.xml's.
             Every other native knob — the initialize-at-run-time entries, the builder image, the
             resource includes — is NOT restated here; it is authored once in
             api-sheriff/src/main/resources/application.properties and reaches the consumer's
             augmentation from the dependency JAR. -->
        <profile>
            <id>native</id>
            <properties>
                <quarkus.native.container-build>true</quarkus.native.container-build>
                <quarkus.native.enabled>true</quarkus.native.enabled>
                <!-- Build-time GraalVM knob enabling SSL support in the native image. Fixed when the
                     image is built, so it is deliberately outside the neutral runtime configuration
                     surface — gateway.yaml is read at startup and could never make it take effect.
                     See ADR-0025 for the boundary rule. -->
                <quarkus.ssl.native>true</quarkus.ssl.native>
            </properties>
        </profile>
    </profiles>
</project>
